# Exercise: Real Echo Server
#
# This is the capstone. By the end of this exercise you have written
# a working TCP echo server and exercised it against a working TCP
# client — both ends running cooperatively in the same program.
#
# The shape (don't memorize — read it once and then write it):
#
#   1. Main listens on a port.
#   2. Main spawns a strand that runs `echo-server` against the
#      listen socket. (Copy-on-spawn gives the spawned strand its
#      own copy of the socket integer; both reference the same
#      kernel handle.)
#   3. Main drops its copy of the listen socket — the spawned
#      strand is now the sole owner.
#   4. Main connects to "127.0.0.1" on that port, writes "ping",
#      and reads the response.
#   5. The spawned `echo-server` accepts the connection (unparks
#      when main's connect arrives), reads "ping", writes it back,
#      and closes both server and client sockets.
#   6. Main asserts the response is exactly "ping" and closes its
#      end.
#
# Strand cooperation: every `net.tcp.*` parking point gives the
# scheduler an opportunity to run the other strand. The data
# dependencies enforce the ordering — accept can't return until
# main connects; main's read can't return until echo-server's
# write completes.
#
# Your task: complete the test-echo body. The echo-server word
# is already written for you (study it; it's the canonical shape
# you'll write again). The piece you fill in is the client side
# in main: connect, write, read, assert, close.
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

# seq:allow(unchecked-tcp-write)
: echo-server ( Socket -- )
    dup net.tcp.accept
    [ # ( server client )
      dup net.tcp.read
      [ # ( server client data )
        over net.tcp.write drop
      ]
      [ drop ]
      if
      net.tcp.close drop
      net.tcp.close drop
    ]
    [ drop net.tcp.close drop ]
    if
;

# seq:allow(unchecked-tcp-write)
: test-echo ( -- )
    18265 net.tcp.listen
    [ # ( server )
      [ echo-server ] strand.spawn drop
      drop                            # spawned strand now owns the listener

      # Your code here: open the client side of the echo.
      #   1. net.tcp.connect to "127.0.0.1" 18265
      #   2. on success: write "ping", read, assert response is "ping",
      #      close the client socket. Use a `[ ok ] [ err ] if` for
      #      both write/read and the connect itself.
      #   3. on failure: drop the junk socket and fail the test.
      false test.assert                # placeholder
    ]
    [ drop ]
    if
;
